statement:
Let $f$ be a function defined from $\{(x,y) : x, y \in \mathbb{R},\, xy \neq 0\}$ to the set of all positive real numbers such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $f(xy, z) = f(x, z) \cdot f(y, z)$ for all $x, y \neq 0$;
\item[(ii)] $f(x, yz) = f(x, y) \cdot f(x, z)$ for all $x, y \neq 0$;
\item[(iii)] $f(x, 1 - x) = 1$ for all $x \neq 0, 1$.
\end{enumerate}
Prove that
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $f(x, x) = f(x, -x) = 1$ for all $x \neq 0$;
\item[(b)] $f(x, y) \cdot f(y, x) = 1$ for all $x, y \neq 0$.
\end{enumerate}
